  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Doctors told the King a woman was birthing rabbits. Godalming, Surrey, autumn 1726. Mary Toft, a clothier's wife, twenty-five years old.",
        "visual_query": "english village surrey countryside",
        "visual_fallback": "country village"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A Guildford surgeon delivered nine of the creatures. London's medical elite came down to watch, and George the First sent his own surgeons.",
        "visual_query": "georgian london street buildings",
        "visual_fallback": "old street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "One of them, Cyriacus Ahlers, examined a rabbit and found corn, hay and straw in its gut. A newborn had been eating hay?",
        "visual_query": "wild rabbit grass meadow",
        "visual_fallback": "wild rabbit"
      },
      {
        "narration": "On the fourth of December a nobleman found out Toft's husband had been buying young rabbits. She confessed three days later.",
        "visual_query": "candlelit desk quill letter",
        "visual_fallback": "quill writing"
      },
      {
        "narration": "She had hoped for fame and a royal pension. Surgeon John Howard was fined eight hundred pounds, and Hogarth put the whole circus into a print.",
        "visual_query": "old courtroom wooden interior",
        "visual_fallback": "court bench"
      }
    ],
